let __v_65 = Value::from("An itch around the vulva with no discharge is usually something touching the skin rather than an infection: perfumed soap or shower gel, bubble bath, wet wipes, washing powder, fabric conditioner, a panty liner, tight or synthetic knickers. Wash with plain water and no soap, wear cotton and loose clothing, change out of damp gym or swim things quickly, and skip perfumed products for a fortnight - most of it settles in days. If there is also a thick white discharge it is more likely thrush, which a pharmacy can treat with a clotrimazole pessary or cream, or a fluconazole tablet if you are not pregnant. See a GP within a couple of weeks if the skin looks white and thin, splits or tears, there is a lump or bleeding, it hurts to sit, or it has not settled after a treatment: a skin condition like lichen sclerosus needs a steroid cream, and it is often missed for years. If there is also a fever, or the skin is swollen and very painful rather than itchy, get seen the same day rather than in a fortnight.");
